// Plain words for this work, by passage key. See ./index.ts.

export const PLAIN_BOOK_OF_THE_DEAD: Record<string, string> = {
  "egy.bd.1": "This is the opening chapter of the spells for \"coming forth by day\" and for going into and out of the underworld, to be said on the day of burial. Ani greets Osiris and lists all the ways he has served him: he says he is one of the gods who help Osiris win against his enemies on the day of judgement, that he has fought for Osiris and Horus, and that he has taken part in Osiris's festivals and rites as a priest. He then asks the gods who bring good souls into the Hall of Osiris to let him in with them, so he can hear, see, stand and sit as they do. He asks those who hand out bread and beer to give him his share, and those who open the roads to open them for him, so that he can come and go freely and not be turned away. He ends by saying he was not found lacking, and the scales have cleared him.",
  "egy.bd.1b": "This chapter is for taking the mummy into the underworld on the day of burial. The dead man, here the scribe Nekht-Amen, calls on the god who lives in Amenta (the land of the dead) and asks to be saved from the worms in Re-stau that eat bodies and drink blood. He says he knows the names of the worms and asks a god of light to come down and swallow them. Part of the text is missing. He then describes himself arriving with a decree from the Lord of All: nobles stand for him, scribes praise him, princes wrap his bandages, and heaven and earth are his, because he is Ra, the first-born of the gods. A closing note says the words are to be said once the dead man is laid to rest.",
  "egy.bd.2": "This chapter is for coming forth by day and living after death. Ani calls twice to the \"Only One\" who shines from the Moon, asking to be allowed out among the crowds around him, to live among the shining ones, and to have the underworld opened to him, so that he can come out by day and do as he wishes on earth among the living.",
  "egy.bd.3": "Another chapter like the one before it (Chapter 2).",
  "egy.bd.4": "Another spell for travelling along the road over the earth.",
  "egy.bd.5": "A spell so that the dead person does not have to do work in the next world.",
  "egy.bd.6": "A spell to make the ushabtiu figures (small servant figures) do the work for a person in the next world.",
  "egy.bd.7": "A spell for passing over the back of Apep, the evil one.\n\nOther possible readings:\n- Literally crossing over the serpent's back.\n- Getting past Apep, or getting the better of him.",
  "egy.bd.8": "This chapter is for passing through Amenta (the land of the dead) and coming forth by day. Ani says the hour opens, Thoth's head is sealed and the eye of Horus is whole; he says he has rescued the shining eye of Horus on Ra's forehead. He says he is Osiris living in Amenta, and he is the Moon among the gods who will never come to an end. He tells Horus to stand up, because Osiris has counted him among the gods.\n\nOther possible readings:\n- \"The hour opens\" is uncertain: Budge marks \"hour\" with a question mark, so something else may be said to open.\n- \"Thoth's head is sealed\" may mean shut up and closed, or sealed in the sense of made safe.",
  "egy.bd.9": "This chapter is for coming forth by day after passing through the tomb. Ani greets a mighty Soul and says he has come through the underworld, seen his father Osiris and driven away the darkness. He says he is Osiris's beloved son, that he has stabbed Set to the heart, done what his father needed, and opened every road in heaven and on earth. Now powerful and fully equipped, he asks all the gods and shining ones to make way for him.",
  "egy.bd.11": "A spell for coming out to face one's enemies in the next world.",
  "egy.bd.12": "Another spell for going into the next world and coming back out of it.",
  "egy.bd.13": "A spell for going into Amentet and coming back out of it.",
  "egy.bd.14": "A spell to drive shame away from the dead person's heart.",
  "egy.bd.15": "A hymn praising Ra as he rises over the eastern horizon of the sky.",
  "egy.bd.15.1": "A hymn to Ra as he rises in the east. Ani the scribe greets Ra, who comes as Khepera, maker of the gods. Ra rises and shines, lighting up his mother Nut, who honours him with both hands; the land of Manu welcomes him and Maat embraces him. Ani asks for power, glory and to come out as a living soul. He calls on the gods who weigh heaven and earth and on Tatunen, maker of people, to praise Ra as he rises in his boat; everyone above and below worships him, with Thoth and Maat as his scribes. Ra's enemy is thrown into the fire, bound and crippled, and the rebels will never rise again. The gods rejoice as his light floods the world, and he comes back each day to where he was the day before. Ani prays to see Ra's beauty, to crush the evil one and destroy Apep, to see the sacred fish and boats, with Horus at the helm and Thoth and Maat beside him, and to see the sun and moon every day. He asks that his soul may roam wherever it likes, that his name be read out at the offering table, that he receive offerings like the followers of Horus, a seat in the sun's boat, and a welcome before Osiris.",
  "egy.bd.15.2": "A hymn to Osiris. Ani praises Osiris Un-nefer, the great god of Abydos and king of eternity, eldest son of Nut and Seb, who holds the crook and flail of his fathers. He tells Osiris to rest easy, because his son Horus now sits on his throne. Through Osiris the world grows green; he goes by many names, Seker, Osiris and Un-nefer among them, and lasts for ever. Ani hails him as King of kings and Lord of lords, with a body of gold, a head of blue and emerald light all around him. He asks Osiris to give him glory in heaven, power on earth and victory in the afterlife; to let him sail to Tattu as a living soul and to Abtu as a phoenix; to pass the gates of the underworld without being turned away; and to have bread, food offerings, and a homestead for ever in Sekhet-Aru with wheat and barley.",
  "egy.bd.15.3": "A hymn to Ra at his rising and setting. Ani greets Ra, who rises as Harmachis, and worships him as his rays fall on his body. Ra sails in peace in his morning and evening boats with fair winds; his enemies are struck down, and the stars that never rest or set sing his praise as he sinks in the west. Ani calls him Ra when he rises and Tmu when he sets, king of the gods, honoured by Nut and held by unchanging order morning and evening. The Fiend has fallen, his arms cut off and his body hacked apart, and Ra's boat sails safely into port. Gods of every direction praise him as the source of all life; when he speaks the earth falls silent. He is the only One, who lived in heaven before the earth and mountains were made, and who made all that exists. Ani asks to breathe the air from Ra's nostrils and the north wind, to have his shining form made glorious and his soul made strong, and for Ra's light to shine on his body every day. He signs off with his titles as scribe and keeper of the gods' offerings.",
  "egy.bd.15.4": "A hymn to Osiris. The speaker praises Osiris, lord of eternity, of many forms, ruler of the tomb and guide of the underworld. Isis holds him and drives the fiends from his path; when he turns to the west, the dead rise to see him, breathe the air and are at peace. Then comes a litany of nine greetings, each hailing Osiris under a different name or place: lord of the stars, the strider across both horizons, the everlasting soul in Tattu, the wearer of the crown, the lord of the acacia tree who turns back the Fiend, the mighty god of Suten-henen, the lord of Abtu who hates deceit, the one in the boat who brings the Nile from its source, and the maker of the gods and king of the whole land. The speaker ends by asking for a peaceful path, saying he is honest, has not knowingly lied, and has done nothing deceitful.",
  "egy.bd.15.5": "A hymn to Ra rising in the east. Ani greets the sun disk and asks it to shine on him, since he praises it at dawn and dusk, and asks that his soul may travel with it across the sky. He praises Horus of the two horizons, the self-made Khepera, whom all the gods are glad to see; Thoth stands in the bow of his boat to destroy his enemies, and the dead come out to bow to him. Ani asks not to be shut in the tomb or turned back, but to have his body made new, since he worshipped Ra while alive. He marvels that Ra's rays cannot be counted and that in a moment he crosses distances that would take millions of years, ending the night on time. Ani prays to reach the everlasting heaven, join the holy shining ones, and watch Ra set into Nut's arms. He praises Ra for flooding the world with light, giving the Nile flood and gladdening every land, and asks Ra to clear him of sin, set his faults aside, and let him travel happily into Sekhet-Aaru. He is promised that he will cross the sky, join the stars, see Ra in his shrine and the sacred fish, and that the Evil One will be cut down. Ra sails into port, his crew rejoices, Horus keeps watch with Thoth and Maat, and Ani asks to be among them.",
  "egy.bd.15b-1": "A hymn praising Ra as he sets in the land of life.",
  "egy.bd.15b-2": "A hymn praising Ra-Harmachis as he sets over the western horizon of the sky.",
  "egy.bd.15b-3": "Another secret chapter of the tuat, for passing through the hidden places of the next world and seeing the Disk as he sets in Amentet.",
  "egy.bd.16a": "This chapter is only a picture (vignette), with no text.",
  "egy.bd.16b": "This chapter has neither a title nor any text in Budge's list.",
  "egy.bd.17": "This long chapter is for going into and out of the underworld, coming out by day in any form the dead man likes, playing draughts, and coming forth as a living soul. It is built as a string of bold statements, each followed by the question \"Who then is this?\" and one or more explanations, often introduced with \"or, as others say\". Ani speaks as the creator god Tmu and as Ra, who came into being alone in the waters of Nu; he says \"I am Yesterday; I know Tomorrow\", explained as Osiris and Ra. He calls himself the bennu bird in Annu, keeper of the book of what is and what will be, and claims the plumes of the god Amsu. He says he has put away his faults and been purified in two sacred pools, and the text gives their names and explains places, gates, gods and myths, such as the fight between Horus and Set and the healing of the eye of Ra by Thoth. He asks the lords of truth to remove his faults, and begs again and again to be saved from fearsome beings: watchers with knives, a dog-faced devourer of the dead by the Fiery Lake, and a god who carries off souls. The text names these beings in several different ways. Near the end he says he is pure and protected by Isis and Nephthys, and he praises a fearsome god who is the Lady of Flame. Some words are lost in places.",
  "egy.bd.18": "This chapter has no title in the manuscript. The god An-maut-f and then Se-mer-f bring Ani before the gods and ask that he be allowed to stay with them, have food and water, and have a home in the fields of Sekhet-Hetep. Ani himself praises Osiris, saying his heart holds what is right, he has not sinned or lied on purpose, and he asks for food in the tomb, free passage in and out of the underworld, and the sight of the Sun and Moon for ever. Then comes a long series of prayers to Thoth: as he once made Osiris win over his enemies, may he make Ani win too, before the gods of each holy city (Annu, Tattu, Sekhem, Pe and Tep, Abtu, Re-stau and others) on the night of each sacred festival. After each prayer the text names those gods and explains what the festival night means. A closing note says that whoever has this chapter recited for him will come out by day, take whatever forms he wishes, escape every fire, and be free from all evil for ever.",
  "egy.bd.19": "The chapter of the crown of victory.\n\nOther possible readings:\n- \"Crown\" is marked uncertain by Budge; the chapter may be about some other token or emblem of victory.",
  "egy.bd.20": "This chapter has no title in Budge's list.",
  "egy.bd.21": "A spell for giving a person back their mouth in the next world.",
  "egy.bd.22": "This chapter is for giving the dead man back his mouth in the underworld. Ani says he rises from the egg in the hidden land and asks for his mouth so he can speak before the great god of the underworld, and that no god's servants may push back his hand and arm. He says he is Osiris, lord of the mouth of the tomb, and that he has come from the Pool of Fire and put it out. He greets the lord of brightness who lives in darkness, says he is pure and supports him, and asks again for his mouth so he can speak and follow his heart through fire and darkness.",
  "egy.bd.23": "This chapter is for opening the dead man's mouth in the underworld. Ani asks Ptah to open his mouth and the god of his town to loosen the wrappings over it. He asks Thoth, full of spells, to undo the bandages of Set that tie his mouth shut, and Tmu to throw them back at anyone who would bind him. He asks Shu to open his mouth with the iron knife he once used for the gods. He says he is the goddess Sekhet and the great goddess Sah, and asks the gods to block any spell or word spoken against him.",
  "egy.bd.24": "This chapter is for bringing magic spells to the dead man in the underworld. Ani says he is Tmu-Khepera, who gave birth to himself, and that he gathers up the spell from wherever it is and from whoever holds it, \"swifter than greyhounds and fleeter than light\". He calls to the one who tows Ra's boat across the Lake of Fire, who also gathers that spell, which creates living forms, makes the gods out of silence and gives them the warmth of life. He says this spell is now given to him from wherever it is (another version says \"fleeter than a shadow\").\n\nOther possible readings:\n- The spell makes the gods \"in silence\", quietly, rather than out of silence: Budge gives both.\n- The living forms it creates come \"from the mother's thigh\", a phrase Budge marks as uncertain.",
  "egy.bd.25": "This chapter is for helping the dead man remember his name in the underworld. He asks to be given his name in the great Double House and to remember it in the House of Fire on the night when the years and months are counted. He says he is with the Holy One on the eastern side of the sky, and that if any god comes towards him, he will at once call out that god's name.",
  "egy.bd.26": "This chapter is for giving Ani a heart in the underworld. Ani asks for his heart to stay with him, or else he will not eat Osiris's cakes or have boats to sail up and down the Nile. He asks for his mouth to speak, feet to walk and arms to beat his enemy. He asks that heaven's doors open, that the god Seb open his blinded eyes and free his bound feet, that Anubis make his legs firm and that Sekhet lift him up to heaven. He says he is now master of his heart, hands and feet and can do whatever his ka (spirit double) wishes, and that his soul will not be cut off from his body at the gates of the underworld.",
  "egy.bd.27": "This chapter is for stopping a man's heart being taken from him in the underworld. Ani speaks to the beings who carry off and steal hearts, and to the lords of eternity, asking them not to seize his heart or raise evil words against it, because it belongs to the mighty one of many names. A few words are unreadable. He says his heart pleases the gods, that he has power over it and his limbs, that it obeys him and will never leave his body, and he orders it to obey him in the underworld.\n\nOther possible readings:\n- \"He is victorious, he has power over it\" may refer to the mighty one of many names, not to Ani.",
  "egy.bd.28": "This chapter is also for stopping the dead man's heart from being carried off in the underworld. He greets the Lion-god, says he is Un, and asks that the Fighter in Annu not take his heart. He calls on the one who binds Osiris and has seen Set. He says his heart sits weeping before Osiris, and asks that it be granted hidden things and food at the order of the Eight gods. He says his heart has been placed on the altars of Tmu, who leads it to the den of Set, and that \"he\" has given it back to him. The meaning of some lines is unclear, and the chapter ends with a puzzling line about finding and burying a leg and wrappings.\n\nOther possible readings:\n- \"He\" who gave back the heart may be Tmu.\n- \"He\" may be Set, whose den was just named.\n- \"They\" who find and bury the leg and wrappings may be the godlike rulers just named, or others not named.",
  "egy.bd.29": "This is the chapter for keeping a man's heart from being taken in the underworld. Ani tells the messenger of the gods to turn back: has he come to take Ani's living heart? It will not be handed over. Ani says the gods listen to his prayers as he goes forward and bow down wherever they are.",
  "egy.bd.29a": "This chapter is for keeping the dead man's heart from being carried away in the underworld. He says his heart is with him and will never be taken, that he is lord of hearts, lives in what is right and true, and is Horus, a pure heart in a pure body. He asks that his heart not be taken or harmed, that he be given no wounds because of its loss, and that he live on in the bodies of his father Seb and his mother Nut. He says he has done no evil against the gods and has not boasted.",
  "egy.bd.29b": "This chapter goes with a heart-shaped amulet made of carnelian. Ani says he is the Bennu bird, the soul of Ra, who guides the gods into the underworld. Just as souls come out onto earth to do what their ka (spirit double) wishes, so Ani's soul comes out to do the will of his ka.",
  "egy.bd.30a": "This chapter is for keeping a man's heart from being driven away from him in the underworld. The dead man calls his heart \"my mother\" and asks that nothing stand up against him at the judgement, and that no one say he has done wrong in front of the great god, lord of Amenta. He greets his heart and his inner organs, and asks the gods of the clouds to speak well of him to Ra. The chapter ends by saying that even though he lies in the depths of the earth, he is not dead in Amenta but lives there in glory.",
  "egy.bd.30b": "A spell to stop the dead person's heart being driven away from them in the next world.",
  "egy.bd.30b.1": "Ani speaks to his heart, calling it his mother and the source of his being. He asks that nothing stand against him at his judgement, that the Tchatcha not oppose him, and that his heart not turn away from him in front of the keeper of the scales. He calls his heart his ka, which holds his body together and makes his limbs strong, and hopes it will go with him to the happy place he is heading for. He asks the Shenit not to make his name stink and that no lies be told against him before the god. The text then breaks off.",
  "egy.bd.30b.2": "This is the chapter for keeping Ani's heart from being driven away from him in the underworld. Ani speaks to his heart, calling it his mother and the thing that brought him into being. He asks that nothing block him at his judgement, that the Tchatcha not stand against him, and that his heart not part from him before the keeper of the scales. His heart is his ka, holding his body together and strengthening his limbs; he hopes it will come with him to the happy place ahead. He asks that the Shenit not make his name stink.",
  "egy.bd.judgement": "Thoth, judge of the gods, announces the verdict: Osiris Ani's heart has been weighed on the great scales, his soul has spoken for him, and he is found true. No wickedness was found in him; he did not waste temple offerings, harm anyone, or spread evil talk while alive. The gods agree with Thoth: Ani is holy and righteous and has not wronged them. They say the devourer Amemet must not get him, and that he will receive offerings, entry before Osiris, and a homestead for ever in Sekhet-hetepu like the followers of Horus. Horus, son of Isis, then brings Ani before Osiris (Unnefer), says his heart came off the scales righteous, and asks that Ani be given cakes and ale and a place among Horus's followers for ever. Ani himself tells Osiris that he stands before him with no sin, has not knowingly lied or acted falsely, and asks to be like the favoured ones around the god.",
  "egy.bd.31": "A spell for driving off the crocodile that comes to take a person's magic words away from them in the next world.",
  "egy.bd.32": "A spell against the one who comes to take a person's magic words away from them in the next world.",
  "egy.bd.33": "A spell for driving off reptiles of every kind.",
  "egy.bd.34": "A spell so that a person is not bitten by a snake in the hall of the tomb.",
  "egy.bd.35": "A spell so that the dead person is not eaten by worms in the next world.",
  "egy.bd.36": "A spell for driving off the tortoise (apsai).",
  "egy.bd.37": "A spell for driving off the two merti.",
  "egy.bd.38a": "A spell for living on the air that is in the next world.",
  "egy.bd.38b": "A spell for living on air and for driving off the two merti.",
  "egy.bd.39": "A spell for driving off the serpent in the next world.",
  "egy.bd.40": "A spell for driving off the one who eats the ass.",
  "egy.bd.41": "A spell to stop the eyes being hurt in the next world.",
  "egy.bd.42": "A spell to put an end to slaughter in Suten-henen.\n\nOther possible readings:\n- A spell so that the dead man himself is not slaughtered in Suten-henen.",
  "egy.bd.42.1": "A list matching each part of Ani's body to a god. His hair is Nu's, his face Ra's, his eyes Hathor's, his ears Ap-uat's, his lips Anpu's, his teeth Serqet's, and his neck Isis's. His hands belong to the Ram of Tattu, his shoulder to Uatchit, his throat to Mert, his forearms to the lady of Sais, his backbone to Sut, his chest to the lords of Kher-aba, and his flesh to the Mighty One of terror. His lower back is Sekhet's, his buttocks the Eye of Horus's, his genitals Osiris's, his legs Nut's, his feet Ptah's, his fingers Orion's, and his leg-bones those of the living serpents.\n\nOther possible readings:\n- The text literally says his throat is \"the blood of Mert\", not Mert's throat.",
  "egy.bd.42.2": "The chapter for driving back slaughter in Suten-henen. The speaker greets the holy place and calls himself 'the Child' four times, says he is Ra and the sacred knot in the tamarisk tree, brighter than yesterday's sun (a few words are missing here). He then goes through his body part by part, matching each to a god (hair to Nu, face to Ra, eyes to Hathor, and so on down to the feet of Ptah), and says no part of him is without its god. Thoth protects him, and no one, whether human, god or dead, can seize him or do him harm. He calls himself yesterday, the 'Seer of millions of years', the eye of the Sun, Horus living for millions of years, the only One born of an only One, and 'Today' for countless peoples. He rules his throne by his words, opens the door of heaven, is reborn each day, and will not die again. He is the plant that grows from Nu with Nut as his mother, unknown yet knowing, unable to be held yet able to hold. He ends by comparing himself to the golden ape in the house of Ptah, without arms or legs. A closing instruction says that after reciting this, you will open a way and go along it.",
  "egy.bd.43": "This chapter is for stopping a man's head being cut off in the underworld. Ani says he is the great one, son of the great one, Fire the son of Fire, who was given back his head after it was cut off. Since Osiris's head was not taken from him, Ani asks that his own not be taken either. He says he has joined his bones together, made himself whole, become young again, and is Osiris, lord of eternity.",
  "egy.bd.44": "This chapter is for not dying a second time in the underworld. Ani says his hiding place has been opened and light shines in the dark. The eye of Horus brought him into being and the god Apuat nursed him; he has hidden among the stars that never set. His brow is like Ra's, his heart is on its throne, he speaks and knows, and he says he is Ra himself. No one scorns or harms him; he is crowned like the king of the gods and will not die again.",
  "egy.bd.45": "This chapter is for not rotting in the underworld. Ani calls out to the one who lies motionless like Osiris, asking that that one's limbs not stay still, not rot, not pass away and not decay, and that it be done for him just as for the god Osiris. A closing note says that if Ani knows this chapter he will not decay in the underworld.\n\nOther possible readings:\n- The motionless one may be Osiris himself.\n- It may be Ani speaking to himself, or to his own body.",
  "egy.bd.46": "This chapter is for not perishing and for coming alive in the underworld. Ani greets the children of Shu, children of the place of dawn and of light, who have taken his crown, and asks that he may rise and go out like Osiris.\n\nOther possible readings:\n- \"His crown\" may be the crown of Shu.\n- It may be the crown of some other god the text does not name.",
  "egy.bd.47": "A spell so that the place of a person's throne is not taken away from them in the next world.\n\nOther possible readings:\n- \"...so that a person's seat, their throne, is not taken away\": Budge gives \"seat\" as another word for \"place\".",
  "egy.bd.48": "This chapter is for a man coming out by day against his enemies in the underworld. Ani says he has split the heavens, passed through the horizon and crossed the earth, following in \"his\" footsteps. He is carried along by the mighty shining ones because he has millions of years full of magic power. He eats and chews with his own mouth, says he is the god who rules the underworld, and asks to be given what lasts for ever without decay.\n\nOther possible readings:\n- \"His\" may be a god he follows, whom the text does not name.\n- \"Following\" is added by Budge; the line may only say he crossed the earth \"upon his footsteps\".",
  "egy.bd.49": "A spell for a person coming out to face their enemies in the next world.",
  "egy.bd.50": "This chapter is for not going to the god's execution block a second time. Ani says Ra has joined together the bones of his neck and back in heaven, on the day he was told to rise from weakness onto his feet. Set and the gods have also joined them as they were before, and he asks that nothing break them apart again and that he be made strong against his father's murderer. He says Nut has put his bones together in their original order, as they were before the gods took visible form. He ends by saying he is Penti, heir of the great gods.",
  "egy.bd.51": "A spell so that the dead person does not walk upside down in the next world.",
  "egy.bd.52": "A spell so that the dead person does not have to eat filth in the next world.",
  "egy.bd.53": "A spell so that a person does not have to eat filth or drink dirty water in the next world.",
  "egy.bd.54": "This chapter is for being given air to breathe in the underworld. Ani says he is the Egg of the Great Cackler (a goose) and guards the great place the god Seb proclaimed on earth. As it lives, he lives; he grows strong and breathes the air. He says he is Utcha-aab, who guards the egg, and that he has foiled Set. He calls on the one in the blue sky who makes the world pleasant with food, asking him to watch over the baby in his cradle when he comes.\n\nOther possible readings:\n- The baby may be the speaker himself, newly born.\n- It may be the young one hatched from the egg he guards.",
  "egy.bd.55": "This is another chapter for being given air to breathe. The dead man says he is Sabsabu and Shu. He breathes in the air before the god of sunbeams out to the furthest ends of heaven and earth, and gives breath to those who are made young again. He opens his mouth and sees with his eyes.",
  "egy.bd.56": "This chapter is for breathing the air on earth. The dead man asks Tmu to give him the sweet breath from his nostrils. He says he holds the mighty throne in Unnu and guards the Egg of the Great Cackler: as he grows, it grows; as he lives, it lives; as he breathes, it breathes.",
  "egy.bd.57": "A spell for breathing in the air and gaining control over the waters in the next world.",
  "egy.bd.58": "This chapter is for breathing the air and having power over the water in the underworld. It is a short dialogue: Ani asks to be let in and is asked who he is and where he is going; he answers that he is one of them and that Merti is with him. He describes being ferried to the temple of the gods \"who have found their faces\", and gives the names of the boat (\"Assembler of Souls\"), its oars, its hold and its rudder. Part of the text is missing. He then asks for milk, cakes, bread, drink and meat in the temple of Anubis. A closing note says that if Ani knows this chapter he can come back in after going out from the underworld.\n\nOther possible readings:\n- The speakers are not marked: Ani may be the one asking the questions, with someone else answering.\n- \"It is Merti\" may be what he is told, not his own answer.\n- \"Found their faces\" is marked uncertain by Budge; the phrase may mean something else.",
  "egy.bd.59": "This chapter is for breathing the air and having power over the water in the underworld. Ani calls on the sycamore tree of the goddess Nut and asks her for the water and air inside her. He says he holds her throne in Unnu and guards the egg of the Great Cackler: as it grows, lives and breathes, so does he.",
  "egy.bd.60": "Spells for drinking water in the next world.",
  "egy.bd.61": "The heading here is for drinking water in the underworld, but the chapter itself is titled for keeping a man's soul from being taken from him there. Ani says he is the one who came out of the flood waters, which he makes rise and swell like the Nile.",
  "egy.bd.62": "Spells for drinking water in the next world.",
  "egy.bd.63a": "This chapter is for drinking water and not being burnt in the fire. The dead man greets the Bull of Amenta and says he is the oar Ra used to ferry the old ones across, and asks not to be buried or burnt up. He says he is Beb, the first-born son of Osiris, the heir, the exalted one, the mighty and calm one, and that he has made his name flourish so that he will live on in memory today.\n\nOther possible readings:\n- \"Exalted\" is marked uncertain by Budge; that title may mean something else.",
  "egy.bd.63b": "This chapter is for not being scalded by water. The dead man says he is the oar ready for rowing that Ra used to ferry the aged gods across. He carries the fluids of Osiris to the lake, away from the flame no one can cross, so that \"he\" is turned aside from its path and not burned. He lies down with the hamemu beings, comes to the Lion's den killing and binding, and follows the path the god came out by.\n\nOther possible readings:\n- The title may mean not being boiled in the water, rather than scalded: Budge gives both.\n- \"He\" who is turned aside may be Osiris, whose fluids are carried away, or the speaker himself.\n- \"The path by which he came forth\" may be the Lion's path or Osiris's.",
  "egy.bd.64": "A spell for coming out by day in the next world.",
  "egy.bd.65": "A spell for coming out by day and getting the better of one's enemies.",
  "egy.bd.66": "A spell for coming out by day.",
  "egy.bd.67": "A spell for opening the doors of the tuat and coming out by day.",
  "egy.bd.68": "A spell for coming out by day.",
  "egy.bd.69": "Another chapter on the same subject as the one before: coming out by day.",
  "egy.bd.70": "Another chapter on the same subject as the ones before: coming out by day.",
  "egy.bd.71": "A spell for coming out by day.",
  "egy.bd.72": "A spell for coming out by day and passing through the hall of the tomb.",
  "egy.bd.72.1": "If the dead man knows this text on earth and it is written on his coffin, he will come out by day in any form he wants, return to his place and not be turned away. Ani will get bread, ale and meat on Osiris's altar and enter the Fields of Aaru in peace, where he will be given wheat and barley, thrive as he did on earth, and do as he pleases like the gods of the underworld, for ever.",
  "egy.bd.72.2": "The chapter for coming out by day and passing through the Ammahet. The dead man greets the everlasting lords of right and truth and asks to come before them. He says he is pure and has power over his spells, has already been judged, and asks to be saved from the crocodile and given back his mouth to speak. He says he knows them and their names and the great god's name. He follows the god Rekem as he travels through the western sky, and asks not to be destroyed, overpowered by the Fiend, or shut out, since he has eaten bread in Pe and drunk ale in Tepu. If his arms are bound, he asks his father Tmu to set up a home for him above the earth with endless wheat and barley, feasts, and every good offering: bread, ale, wine, oxen, ducks, linen, incense and wax. He asks to rise again in any form he likes and sail up and down the fields of Aaru in peace, for he is the double Lion-god.",
  "egy.bd.74": "This chapter is for walking on two legs and coming out onto the earth. Ani tells Seker that he has finished all his work in Ani's legs in the underworld. He says he shines above the Leg of the Sky, comes out of heaven and rests with the glorified spirits. Then he cries out twice that he is weak and feeble; he walks, but he is weak before those who gnash their teeth in the underworld.",
  "egy.bd.75": "A spell for travelling to Annu (On) and being given a home there.",
  "egy.bd.76": "A spell for a person to change into any form they like.",
  "egy.bd.77": "This chapter is for turning into a golden hawk. Ani asks to rise like a golden hawk hatching from its egg, flying and hovering with a back seven cubits wide and wings of southern emeralds. He asks to step from one sacred boat to another, to have his heart brought to him from the eastern mountain, and to have crowds come bowing to him. He asks to go before Ra every day to hear his words, to sit among the mighty gods of Nut, and to have a home with food and drink, where he can eat his fill and shine. He ends by asking for power over the guardian of his head.",
  "egy.bd.78": "A spell for turning into a sacred hawk. Ani asks a mighty god to clear his paths, renew his strength and make him feared, so that the gods of the underworld will fight on his side and no one who means him harm can come near. He says he is one of the shining beings made by Tmu, older and stronger than many, and that he rose up as a sacred hawk whom Horus made like his own soul, to take charge of what belongs to Osiris in the underworld. The double Lion-god at first tells him the nemmes crown is not for him; then Horus tells him what his father said of him, and the words \"I have given you the nemmes\" are spoken. In the end it is tied on his head and a way is opened for him. Ani says he has come from Tattu, seen Osiris, told him news of his son Horus and of a wound in Set's heart, and made the gods understand Horus's plans. He ends by saying he has passed through the underworld, opened the roads of heaven and earth, and has not been turned back.\n\nOther possible readings:\n- \"I have given you the nemmes\" may be Horus speaking to Ani directly.\n- It may be Osiris's own words, as Horus reports what his father said.",
  "egy.bd.79": "A spell for joining the company of the gods and becoming a ruler among them. The dead man greets Tmu, maker of all that grows and father of the gods, and then all the hidden gods of creation, of the flooded lands, of the underworld and of heaven. He asks to be let in, saying he knows them and their secret names and forms, is pure and powerful, and has brought perfume, incense and natron. He asks them to wipe out any grudge they hold against him, since he has ended every wrong he did them. He says he now sits on Ra's throne in the horizon, receives offerings at his altar, and is honoured like the god whom Nut gives birth to every day.",
  "egy.bd.80": "A spell for becoming the god who gives light in the darkness. Ani says he is the girdle of the robe of the god Nu, which shines and sends light into the dark, and that the words of his mouth bring together the two fighters inside him and lift up the one who fell. He says he carried off the god Hu, took the darkness captive, held up the Eye of the Sun when its power was fading before the festival of the fifteenth day, and supplied Thoth for that feast. He describes months of emerald and crystal and a home among sapphire furrows. Calling himself the Lady who sheds light, he says he has lit up the blackness, overthrown the destroyers and raised up those who wept with their faces hidden, and they looked at him. He ends: \"I am the Lady, and I will not let you hear concerning me.\"\n\nOther possible readings:\n- The fallen one may be the god himself, or the one \"who was with him in the valley of Abtu\"; the text names neither.\n- The last line may mean she keeps herself hidden, or that she lets no talk about her reach them.",
  "egy.bd.81a": "A spell for turning into a lotus (Budge's title calls it a lily). Ani says he is the pure lotus that comes out of the god of light, guardian of the nostrils of Ra and of the nose of Hathor. He says he hurries after Horus, and that he is the pure one who comes up out of the field.\n\nOther possible readings:\n- The guardian of Ra's nostrils and Hathor's nose may be the god of light.\n- It may be the lotus itself, that is, Ani.",
  "egy.bd.81b": "A spell for turning into a lily.",
  "egy.bd.82": "A spell for turning into Ptah, for eating cakes and drinking ale, for freeing the body and for living in Annu (On). Ani says he eats bread, drinks ale, puts on clothes, flies like a hawk and honks like a goose. He says he has never eaten anything foul or forbidden; he lives on the bread of the gods, eating it in the shade of Hathor's tree, and he makes offerings in Tattu and Annu. His head is like Ra's, his tongue like Ptah's, his throat like Hathor's, and he speaks the words of his father Tmu. He is counted heir of Seb, lord of the earth; the people of Annu bow to him as their bull, and he grows stronger by the moment for millions of years.",
  "egy.bd.83": "A spell for turning into a bennu bird (Budge's title calls it a phoenix). Ani says he came into being out of shapeless matter, made himself in the image of the god Khepera, grew like a plant, and is made from pieces of all the gods. He calls himself the 'yesterday' of the four quarters of the world and the seven cobras that appeared in the east. He says he sails among the gods, has been crowned, has become shining, mighty and holy, and is Khonsu, who drives back everything that opposes him.",
  "egy.bd.84": "A spell for turning into a heron. Ani says he has charge of the animals brought for sacrifice, and that \"he makes slaughter on earth, and I make slaughter on earth\"; and that he has gone along the high road to heaven, purified himself and walked with long strides to his city. He says he has put the gods in their places, honoured their temples, and knows Nut, Tatunen, Teshert and Heka; he is 'the red calf drawn with the pen', and the gods bow and invite him in. He has spoken no evil in the place of truth, honoured Osiris and feared no terrors. He calls himself Nu and Shu, a soul that is eternity, creator of darkness, a lord of millions of years whose nest is in the far reaches of heaven and whose egg is unbroken. The passage ends with a wish that Ani's body may stay in Annu and be seen in the burial place in Amenta; the text then breaks off.\n\nOther possible readings:\n- \"He\" may be Osiris Ani himself, named just before, spoken of in the third person.\n- \"He\" may be another god whose slaughter Ani matches.",
  "egy.bd.85": "A spell for turning into a soul, here the soul of Tmu, and for not entering the place of punishment; the title says whoever knows it will never perish. Ani says he has not gone into the house of destruction, has not been wiped out and has not rotted. He says he is Ra coming out of Nu, a divine soul who made his own body; he hates wrongdoing, does not even look at it, and lives by what is right and true. He is the god Hu who never dies as a soul, who came into being with Nu as Khepera, and he is the lord of light.",
  "egy.bd.86": "A spell for turning into a swallow; it opens by saying this is where the chapters of transformations begin. Ani says he is the swallow and the scorpion, daughter of Ra, and greets the sweet-smelling gods and the flame rising from the horizon. He asks to be led to the Island of Flame and says he has come with his warrant, so the doors should open. He tells how Horus was given his father's throne while Sut got the fall he had caused, and how he himself was judged, found pure and freed of his faults and sins. He says he has come out by day walking on his own legs, knows the hidden ways to the gates of the Field of Aaru, and asks that, though his body is buried, he may rise and defeat all his enemies on earth.",
  "egy.bd.87": "A spell for turning into the serpent Seta. Ani says he is the serpent Seta, very old, living at the edges of the earth. Each day he lies down and is born again, renewing himself and growing young.",
  "egy.bd.88": "A spell for turning into a crocodile. Ani says he is the frightening, sacred crocodile that brings destruction, and the great fish in Kamui. He says he is the lord who is worshipped in Sekhem, and that Ani is that lord.",
  "egy.bd.89": "A spell for reuniting the soul with its body in the underworld. Ani calls on the god Annitu and the Runner to bring his soul back to him from wherever it is, and asks the Eye of Horus to raise him up like those who never lie down in death. He asks not to lie dead in Annu, where souls are joined to their bodies by the thousands. Parts of the text are lost here. He then asks the gods who tow the sun's boat across the underworld, and who put souls back into their bodies, to destroy the enemy and let his soul come out triumphant from the eastern horizon, see its body, rest in it, and never perish or decay. A closing note says the words are to be spoken over a golden figure of a soul set with precious stones, placed on the dead man's neck.",
  "egy.bd.90": "A spell for giving a person their memory.",
  "egy.bd.91": "A spell for keeping a person's soul from being shut in the underworld. Ani praises an exalted, feared Ram, crowned on his great throne, who makes the way for Ani's spirit and soul. Ani says he is fully equipped and has made his way to where Ra and Hathor are. A closing note says that whoever knows this chapter will be a fully equipped shining spirit in the underworld and will not be stopped at any door, going in and out millions of times.\n\nOther possible readings:\n- \"Ram\" may be read \"Soul\": Budge gives both, so he may be praising a mighty Soul rather than a ram.",
  "egy.bd.92": "A spell for opening the tomb to the soul and shadow, so the dead man can come out by day and use his legs. Ani says the place where he was held is opened for his soul by order of the Eye of Horus; his strides are long and his limbs strong, and as Horus, avenger of his father, he sets the crown in its place. He asks that his soul and shadow not be locked in, so his soul can see the great god in Ra's boat on the day souls are judged. He asks that the hidden beings who tie up the limbs of Osiris and imprison souls and shadows do him no harm and turn away from him. He tells someone \"your heart is with you\", and ends by asking: \"The place you hold, is it not heaven?\" A closing note says whoever knows this chapter will come out by day and his soul will not be shut in.\n\nOther possible readings:\n- \"You\" may be Osiris.\n- \"You\" may be Ani's own soul, being reassured.",
  "egy.bd.93": "A spell for not being carried off to the east in the underworld. Ani calls on the virile power of Ra, which pushes down all opposition, and says it makes him stronger than the strong. So, he says, he will not be dragged east to the demons' festivals, cut with knives, hemmed in, or gored by horns. The text breaks off at this point.",
  "egy.bd.93a": "Another chapter. Ani says that no fiends will harm him, he will not be gored by the horns of Khepera, and the power of Ra will not be swallowed. He goes into his homestead and gathers his harvest, and the gods talk with him. He tells Ra-khepera not to gore them, and says the eye of Tmu will not fall sick or be destroyed. He asks to be \"brought to an end\", and not to be carried off to the East to the feasts of the fiends who are his enemies, nor cut cruelly, and declares that he, Ani the scribe, is not taken away to the East. The opening of the text is missing.\n\nOther possible readings:\n- \"Them\" whom Ra-khepera is told not to gore may be the gods who talk with him, or the fiends.\n- \"Let me be brought to an end\" may mean let me reach the end of my journey, not that his life be ended.",
  "egy.bd.94": "A spell for asking for an ink jar and a writing palette.",
  "egy.bd.95": "A spell for being close to Thoth.",
  "egy.bd.96": "A spell for being close to Thoth, and for giving… (the rest of the title is missing).",
  "egy.bd.97": "A spell for being close to Thoth, and for giving… (the rest of the title is missing).",
  "egy.bd.98": "The title of this chapter is incomplete in Budge's list.",
  "egy.bd.99": "A spell for bringing a boat in the next world.",
  "egy.bd.100": "A spell for making the khu perfect and getting it into the boat of Ra, along with his divine followers.",
  "egy.bd.101": "A spell for protecting the boat of Ra.",
  "egy.bd.102": "A spell for going on board the boat of Ra.",
  "egy.bd.103": "A spell for being among the followers of Hathor.",
  "egy.bd.104": "A spell for sitting among the great gods.",
  "egy.bd.105": "A spell for keeping the ka satisfied.",
  "egy.bd.106": "A spell for giving a person joy every day in Het-ka-Ptah (Memphis).",
  "egy.bd.107": "A spell for going in and out through the gate of the gods of the west among the god's followers, and for knowing the souls of Amentet.",
  "egy.bd.108": "A spell for knowing the souls of the West.",
  "egy.bd.109": "A spell for knowing the souls of the East.",
  "egy.bd.110": "The opening of the chapters about the Fields of Peace and the Field of Reeds, and about going into and coming out of the underworld. The wish is to be strong there, to plough, reap, eat, drink and love there, just as on earth. Ani then says Set carried Horus off to see what was being built in the Field of Peace, that \"he\" spreads air over the divine soul in the egg and rescues the inner body of Horus, and that he himself has crowned \"him\" in the House of Shu and sailed in the great boat on the Lake of Peace towards its cities and the city of Hetep. He has calmed the two holy Fighters who guard life by doing right and bringing an offering, defeated their enemies and ended the troubles of their children. He asks to control his own mouth with spells, not to be overpowered by demons, and to be provided for in the Fields of Peace; the god answers that he may do whatever he wishes.\n\nOther possible readings:\n- \"He\" who spreads the air and rescues Horus's body may be Set.\n- \"He\" may be Horus or another god.\n- \"Him\" whom Ani crowns may be Horus or Set.",
  "egy.bd.112": "A spell for knowing the souls of Pe.",
  "egy.bd.113": "A spell for knowing the souls of Nekhen.",
  "egy.bd.114": "A spell for knowing the souls of Khemennu (Hermopolis).",
  "egy.bd.115": "A spell for going up to the sky, passing through the hall of the tomb, and knowing the souls of Annu.",
  "egy.bd.116": "A spell for knowing the souls of Annu.",
  "egy.bd.117": "A spell for setting out on a road in Re-stau.",
  "egy.bd.118": "A spell for coming out of Re-stau.",
  "egy.bd.119": "A spell for knowing the name of Osiris, and for going into Re-stau and coming back out.",
  "egy.bd.122": "A spell for the dead person to go back in after coming out of the next world.",
  "egy.bd.123": "A spell for going into the great house (the tomb).",
  "egy.bd.124": "A spell for going in to the divine rulers of Osiris. Ani says his soul has built him a house in Tattu, he has grown strong in Pe, and he ploughs his fields where his palm tree stands. He refuses anything foul: his bread is of white grain and his ale of red barley, brought to him by boat, and he eats it under trees he knows well. He asks the doorkeeper to bring him those who make offerings, and asks that the shining ones welcome him and the gods make him triumphant and powerful in heaven. He says he goes in and out through the sun-disk, talks with the gods and shining ones, and acts as go-between, passing men's words to Osiris and the gods' words back. He ends by calling himself a shining one clothed in power, mightier than any other.",
  "egy.bd.125": "The words the dead person is to say on reaching the hall of Maati, which separates them from their sins and lets them see God, the Lord of mankind.",
  "egy.bd.125.1": "The chapter for entering the Hall of Right and Truth. Ani tells Osiris he has come to see his beauty and raises his hands to his name. He has reached a barren place with no trees or grass, entered the place of hidden things, and spoken with Sut (part of the text is missing here). Anubis says Ani knows their roads and cities and smells like one of them. Ani lists where he has been and what he has done: he visited holy places, made offerings, sank his enemies' boat, sailed the sacred lake, kept silent in Tattu, entered Re-stau and the House of Osiris and saw their secrets (there is another gap). Anubis quizzes him on the names of the door and its two leaves; Ani answers correctly, and Anubis lets him pass. Then comes the 'negative confession': Ani addresses forty-two gods one by one, each named or described and linked to a place, and to each he denies a wrong. He says he has not done evil, robbed, stolen, killed, cheated the offerings, lied, snatched food, caused pain or tears, committed adultery, laid waste farmland, eavesdropped, been angry without cause, caused terror, raged, ignored the truth, stirred up strife, judged hastily, talked too much, cursed the king or God, fouled the water, spoken scornfully, taken food from a child, or killed the god's cattle with evil intent.",
  "egy.bd.125.2": "Instructions: Ani is dressed in fine clothes and white sandals, anointed with precious ointment, and given offerings of a bull, herbs, incense, ducks, flowers, ale and cakes. You should paint a table of offerings in clean colours on a clean tile and bury it in a field where pigs have not walked. If these words are written on it, the dead man will rise again and his descendants will thrive like Ra. He will be favoured among the king's chiefs, receive cakes, drink and meat from the great god's table, never be turned away from any door in the west, travel with the kings of north and south, and stay with the followers of Osiris for ever.",
  "egy.bd.126": "This chapter has no title in Budge's list.",
  "egy.bd.127a": "The book of praise for the gods of the qerti.",
  "egy.bd.127b": "Words to be spoken when going to the chiefs of Osiris, praising the gods who lead in the tuat.",
  "egy.bd.128": "A chapter of praise to Osiris.",
  "egy.bd.130": "A spell for making the khu perfect.",
  "egy.bd.131": "A spell for letting a person go up into the sky to be at the side of Ra.",
  "egy.bd.132": "A spell for letting a man come back to see his home on earth. Ani says he is the Lion-god striding out, who has shot arrows and wounded the prey. He says he is the Eye of Horus, has opened it at its hour, and has come to the furrows, and he asks that Ani may come in peace.",
  "egy.bd.133": "A spell for making the spirit perfect in the underworld before the great company of the gods, to be said on a set day of the month. Ani describes Ra rising from his hidden place in the eastern horizon at Nut's word, followed by his gods, renewed every day, breathing the breezes and turning his face to the beautiful west. He asks that the gods of heaven praise Ani as they praise Ra, that he be given his share of food and drink, be strong on earth and below it, and rise each day like Ra, never lying still in the earth, seeing and hearing what is true. He says Ani has not revealed what he saw or heard in the hidden house, and that he is a god, the flesh of Ra, at peace in the boat of Nu. A closing note says to recite this over a green boat seven cubits long with a starry sky, an image of Ra at the front and an image of the dead man in it; it must be shown to no one but yourself, your father or your son, and the dead man will be seen in the underworld as Ra's messenger.",
  "egy.bd.134": "A spell for boarding the boat of Ra and joining his followers, sung as a hymn to Ra on a set day of the month. Ani praises Ra rising in his boat, gladdening people for millions of years, and having beaten Apepi. He calls on the children of Seb to destroy his enemies: Horus will cut off their heads, and they will fall to earth as beasts and into the water as fish. Ani destroys every hostile demon in heaven, on earth or in the water, and becomes second to Ra. Isis gives birth to him and Nephthys nurses him, as they did for Horus, and everyone, gods, men and the damned, must fall on their faces when they see him crowned and victorious. A closing note says to recite this over a hawk wearing the white crown, write the names of nine gods in green on a new tablet placed in a boat with a figure of the dead man, burn incense and roast ducks; then the dead man will travel with Ra wherever he sails, and Ra's enemies will be slain.",
  "egy.bd.135": "Another chapter, to be recited every month when the moon is growing.",
  "egy.bd.136a": "A spell for sailing in the boat of Ra.",
  "egy.bd.136b": "A spell for sailing in the great boat of Ra and travelling round the sun's fiery path.",
  "egy.bd.137a": "A spell for lighting the fire that is to be made in the next world.",
  "egy.bd.137b": "A spell for the dead person to light the fire.",
  "egy.bd.138": "A spell for letting the dead person enter Abydos.",
  "egy.bd.140": "A book to be recited in the second month of pert, when the utchat is full.",
  "egy.bd.141": "A book a man is to recite for his father and his son at the festivals of Amentet, on the ninth day of the festival. It will make him perfect before Ra and the gods, and he will live with them.\n\nOther possible readings:\n- \"Him\" may be the man who recites it.\n- \"Him\" may be the father or son it is recited for.",
  "egy.bd.142": "A book a man is to recite for his father and his son at the festivals of Amentet, on the ninth day of the festival. It will make him perfect before Ra and the gods, and he will live with them.\n\nOther possible readings:\n- \"Him\" may be the man who recites it.\n- \"Him\" may be the father or son it is recited for.",
  "egy.bd.143": "A book a man is to recite for his father and his son at the festivals of Amentet, on the ninth day of the festival. It will make him perfect before Ra and the gods, and he will live with them.\n\nOther possible readings:\n- \"Him\" may be the man who recites it.\n- \"Him\" may be the father or son it is recited for.",
  "egy.bd.144": "A spell for going in.",
  "egy.bd.145a": "This chapter has no title in Budge's list.",
  "egy.bd.145b": "A spell for coming out to the hidden pylons (gateways).",
  "egy.bd.146": "A spell for knowing the gateways (pylons) of the house of Osiris in the Field of Aaru. At each of ten gateways Ani describes the goddess of that gate and names its doorkeeper. The first is a lady of terrors with high walls who rescues travellers from destruction (doorkeeper Neruit); the second, a fire-devouring lady of heaven (Mes-Ptah); the third, a lady of the altar who receives offerings, beloved of every god (Sebaq); the fourth, a lady armed with knives who destroys the Still-Heart's enemies (Nekau). The fifth is a flame, lady of breath for the nostrils, that cannot be approached (Hentet-Arqiu); the sixth, a lady of light of unknown size with a vast serpent above her (Semati); the seventh, a robe that weeps over the dead man it covers (Sakti-f); the eighth, a blazing fire no one can pass through (Khu-tchet-f). The ninth is a great lady clothed in green stone who clothes the weak (Ari-su-tchesef), and the tenth a loud-voiced, fearless lady who makes those who plead with her cry out (Sekhen-ur).\n\nOther possible readings:\n- \"Lady of breath\" and \"beloved of every god\" are both marked uncertain by Budge.\n- The broken line at the fifth gate may mean no one may come forward to plead with her, or that whoever pleads with her will not reach her.",
  "egy.bd.147": "A spell for the dead man to say as he reaches the halls of Amentet. For each of seven halls the text names the doorkeeper, the watcher and the herald, and gives Ani's words there. At the first he says he is a mighty one who makes his own light, praises Osiris and asks not to be driven away from the wall of burning coals. At the second he speaks of one who weighs words like Thoth, and asks to pass and see Ra. At the third and fourth he says he has cleared away the offences of Osiris, eased his pain and opened the way in Re-stau, and as the son of Osiris has passed into the realm of the dead. At the fifth he tells Osiris he has brought him his jawbones and backbone, put his limbs together and driven off Apep. Part of the sixth hall's text is missing; what remains says he avenged and bound up the Eye of Osiris. At the seventh he greets Osiris, cleansed, who travels round heaven in the boat, says that \"his body\" grows strong and lives at his words, and asks him to make all the roads to him easy.\n\nOther possible readings:\n- At the second hall, \"he\" who weighs words as the second of Thoth may be a god, or Ani speaking of himself.\n- At the seventh, \"his body\" may be the body of Osiris or of Ra.",
  "egy.bd.148": "A spell for feeding the spirit in the underworld and keeping every evil thing away from him. Ani greets Ra, lord of truth and of eternity, and says he has made offerings to the seven cows and their bull. He asks those who give cakes and ale to the shining ones to let his soul be with them, to be born on their laps, and to become a glorious being in the beautiful west. He then greets the four beautiful rudders of the northern, western, eastern and southern sky, and the four groups of gods: the gods and mother-gods who are above the earth and in the underworld, the pilots of the underworld, and the followers of Ra in the train of Osiris.",
  "egy.bd.149": "This chapter has no title in Budge's list.",
  "egy.bd.150": "This chapter has no title in Budge's list.",
  "egy.bd.151": "This passage has no chapter title of its own; it gives the words spoken by the protective figures around the dead man. Isis says she has come to guard him, giving him the north wind for breath and making him like a god, with his enemies under his feet. Nephthys says she circles round to protect her brother Osiris. The flames of Isis and Nephthys say they drive the enemy from the tomb and will not let him be cut to pieces. The Tet pillar says it has driven off the god with the hidden face. The four sons of Horus, Mestha, Hapi, Tuamautef and Qebhsennuf, each say they have come to protect Ani: putting his head and limbs together, crushing his enemies, gathering his bones and putting his heart back in its place. Two birds praise Ra as he sets and rises, and the perfected soul calls itself a great cat. Finally Ani tells his shabti figure that if he is ordered to do work in the underworld, such as ploughing, watering the channels or carrying sand, it should do it, and the figure answers that it is here and will do it when called.",
  "egy.bd.151a": "The chapter of the hands of Anpu, who lives in the burial chamber, being laid on the lord of life (the mummy).",
  "egy.bd.151b": "The chapter of the chief of hidden things.",
  "egy.bd.152": "A spell for building a house in the earth.",
  "egy.bd.153a": "A spell for getting out of the net.",
  "egy.bd.153b": "A spell for getting out of the fishing net.",
  "egy.bd.154": "A spell to stop a person's body rotting in the tomb.",
  "egy.bd.155": "A spell for a golden Tet amulet to be placed on the spirit's neck. Ani calls on the 'still heart', saying it rises and shines, and asks it to stand at his side. He says he has brought it a golden Tet and tells it to rejoice in it.",
  "egy.bd.156": "A spell for a buckle amulet to be placed on the spirit's neck; Budge's title calls it amethyst, but the spell itself says carnelian. Ani says the blood, the spells and the power of Isis protect him and crush whatever he hates.",
  "egy.bd.157": "The chapter of a gold vulture to be placed on the neck of the khu.",
  "egy.bd.158": "The chapter of a gold collar to be placed on the neck of the khu.",
  "egy.bd.159": "The chapter of a sceptre of mother-of-emerald to be placed on the neck of the khu.",
  "egy.bd.160": "The chapter of putting in place a plaque of mother-of-emerald.",
  "egy.bd.161": "The chapter of Thoth opening the doors of the sky (Budge's title is shortened with \"etc.\").",
  "egy.bd.162": "A spell for making warmth under the head of the khu.",
  "egy.bd.163": "A spell to stop a person's body rotting in the next world.",
  "egy.bd.164": "Another chapter, following the one before it on keeping the body from rotting in the next world.",
  "egy.bd.165": "A spell for reaching port, for not becoming invisible, for making the body sprout, and for refreshing it with the water of the sky.",
  "egy.bd.166": "A spell for the headrest placed under the dead man's head, to keep harm away from his body. The speaker tells Osiris to lift his head to the sky, because he has been put back together and made triumphant. Ptah has overthrown the enemies of Osiris, and they will never get up again.",
  "egy.bd.167": "A spell for bringing the utchat.",
  "egy.bd.168a": "This chapter has no title in Budge's list.",
  "egy.bd.168b": "This chapter has no title in Budge's list.",
  "egy.bd.169": "A spell for setting up the offering chamber.",
  "egy.bd.170": "The chapter of the roof of the offering chamber.",
  "egy.bd.171": "A spell for tying on the abu.",
  "egy.bd.172": "This is where the praises to be recited in the next world begin.",
  "egy.bd.173": "Words spoken by Horus to his father.",
  "egy.bd.174": "A spell for letting the khu come out through the great gate of the sky.",
  "egy.bd.175": "A spell for not dying a second time in the underworld. Ani asks Thoth what has happened to the children of Nut, who have fought, done evil, created demons and killed, the strong always working against the weak. He asks Thoth to carry out Tmu's decree, and says he is Thoth's writing palette and ink jar and has done no secret wrong. Then he asks Tmu what this land is: without water or air, bottomless and pitch-black, where no one can have peace of heart or satisfy love. He asks to be given the state of the shining ones in place of water, air and love, and calm of heart in place of bread and ale. He asks how long he will live, and is told millions of millions of years. The speaker then tells of Horus inheriting the throne of Osiris and of the soul of Sut being sent away; part of the text is lost here. Ani ends by asking to last on earth, keep his throne, have a strong heir, see his tomb and friends flourish and his enemies destroyed, and to have life, strength and health.\n\nOther possible readings:\n- These lines may be spoken by Tmu, answering Ani.\n- They may be Ani's own words, speaking as a god (\"I am Fate and Osiris\").\n- \"Fate\" is marked uncertain by Budge.",
  "egy.bd.176": "A spell for not dying a second time in the next world.",
  "egy.bd.177": "A spell for raising up the khu and making the soul live in the next world.",
  "egy.bd.178": "A spell for raising up the body, making the eyes see and the ears hear, setting the head firmly in place and giving it its powers.",
  "egy.bd.179": "A spell for coming out of yesterday, coming out by day, and praying with the hands.",
  "egy.bd.180": "A spell for coming out by day, praising Ra in Amentet, and giving praise to those who are in the tuat.",
  "egy.bd.181": "A spell for going in to the divine chiefs of Osiris, who are the leaders in the tuat.",
  "egy.bd.182": "A book for making the backbone of Osiris firm, giving breath to the one whose heart is still, and Thoth driving off the enemies of Osiris.\n\nOther possible readings:\n- One whose heart has stopped, that is, a dead person.\n- One whose heart is calm or at rest.\n- \"Still-Heart\" as a title, as it is used elsewhere in the book.",
  "egy.bd.183": "A hymn praising Osiris, giving him glory and giving worship to Un-nefer.",
  "egy.bd.184": "A spell for being with Osiris.",
  "egy.bd.185": "A hymn of praise to Osiris, the everlasting lord who lives in Amentet. Ani greets Osiris as lord of eternity, Lord of Lords, King of Kings and God of gods, and asks for a seat among those in the underworld who worship him for millions of years. Part of the text is missing here. Ani then asks that he meet no delay in Ta-mera, that \"they all\", great and small, may come to him, and that his ka may freely go into and out of the underworld without being turned back at its gates.\n\nOther possible readings:\n- \"They\" may be those in the underworld who worship Osiris.\n- The missing text before this line may have named someone else.",
  "egy.bd.186": "A hymn of praise to Hathor, mistress of Amentet, and to Meh-urt. Only the opening survives here, a string of Hathor's titles: lady of Amentet and of the land of the dead, the Eye of Ra who lives on his brow, and the beautiful face in the Boat of Millions of Years. The rest of the text is broken off.",
};
